[#:seon.task{:name "an-escaped-pass-closes-the-reply-without-its-fault"
             :kind :function
             :intent "When a turn pass escapes after `seon.fault/fault!` committed, turn-step's finally (agent.clj ~515-558) closes the reply and the caller gets `:seon.await/closed-error` with no fault, although the receipt is on the throw's ex-data (`:seon.fault/recorded`). The causing agent meets the fault only in its next, unrelated run. Offer the recorded receipt (or the fault value) on the reply before closing it. Done when a regression whose pass escapes after recording a fault receives that fault as data in the same reply. The same closed channel meets a second trigger: `seon.turn/settle-batch-refusal!` throws when its own write is refused, the pass rethrows through `seon.turn/close-escaped-run!`, and the caller sees `:seon.await/closed-error` while the fault goes only to root and to the agent's next run; return both refusals as the value ({:refused-outcome :refusal-refused}) and answer them on the reply, and boot recovery closes the run as for any failed terminal write (regression: a refusal settlement that is itself refused answers both refusals as data in its own reply)."
             :must-read #{seon.cluster.agent/turn-step seon.cluster.agent/evaluate-source! seon.fault/carried-receipt seon.turn/settle-batch-refusal!}
             :evidence #{"docs/research/agent-platform/stability-sweep-2026-09-27.md"}}
 #:seon.task{:name "interrupted-rewalks-the-cause-chain",
            :kind :function,
            :intent
            "`seon.cluster.agent/interrupted?` walks .getCause with its own seen-set loop, a second copy of `seon.error.refusal/cause-links`. Replace the loop with (some #(instance? InterruptedException %) (`seon.error.refusal/cause-links` failure)), EQUIVALENT on every chain (both stop at the first repeat)."}
 #:seon.task{:name "reply-size-prints-sources-to-measure-them",
            :kind :function,
            :intent
            "`seon.cluster.agent/submit-source-in-projection` still computes `:seon.turn/reply-size` by printing sources and counting that text. Carry the size from the authored request text without printing its parsed forms. Keep reply-size's consumer and units explicit; a regression must show that measuring the reply never prints or realizes its values.",
            :must-read #{seon.cluster.agent/submit-source!},
            :evidence #{"docs/research/agent-platform/2026-10-07/plan-research/r-read-notes.md"}}
 #:seon.task{:name "finished-agents-and-their-branches-accumulate"
             :kind :function
             :intent "Default holds 185 agents, each with a `:seon.agent/branch` (measured through JVM eval_clj on default: 185 `:seon.agent/id` entities, 185 branch values), and bin/seon start --head captured about 110 agent branch heads at a restart. Agents from finished lanes and probes are never archived and their branches never unlinked, so every restart captures and restores them. Related: my.agent/retire-branch-refuses-a-finished-agent-as-admission-closed (retire at run exit). Done when a finished agent's branch is unlinked and the agent archived at its run's exit, and the agent count on default after a probe batch returns to its prior value."
             :must-read #{my.agent/retire-branch! seon.cluster.registry/retire-branch!}}
#:seon.task{:name "two-plan-passes-start-one-attempt"
             :kind :function
             :intent "Card R1 (r1-db-flow-design.md, hybrid-start-a-flow.md H6/H7). `seon.cluster.agent/db<->flow` (the one Flow proc in the cluster graph linking Datahike and Flow) reads `seon.cluster.agent/plan-facts` on main (`seon.task/ready`, open attempts with their task's intent, ended counts, need end commits) and the registry, asks the pure `seon.cluster.agent/plan` for actions and applies each: `:attempt` writes the attempt and its agent in ONE main transaction (`seon.task/attempt-tx` + `seon.cluster.agent/creation-tx`) before anything runs, `:start-flow` calls `seon.cluster.agent/start-flow!` (fork from the fork point, world, environment, graph paused, resumed only under :running). A second pass writes nothing and starts nothing; `:seon.config.agent/max-running` (default 4) counts open attempts. Nothing else starts a graph: `seon.cluster.host/host!` and the per-branch db<->flow graph are deleted. Proofs: seon.cluster.db-flow-test/plan-writes-the-attempt-first-and-makes-flow-follow (the table), the two-step chain test."
             :must-read #{seon.cluster.agent/db<->flow seon.cluster.agent/plan seon.cluster.agent/start-flow! seon.flow/start-graph! seon.cluster.registry/fork! seon.task/ready seon.task/attempt-tx seon.context/current-state seon.profile/measure}
             :evidence #{"docs/research/agent-platform/2026-10-11/hybrid-start-a-flow.md" "docs/research/agent-platform/2026-10-11/r1-db-flow-design.md"}}
 #:seon.task{:name "each-intent-names-one-roster-state"
             :kind :function
             :intent "Card R2. Intents to Flow verbs: :paused launches without resume or pauses a running graph; :running resumes or re-hosts; :stopped pauses, joins, stops and releases the SCI context keeping agent, branch and attempt; :cancelled interrupts, ends :cancelled with a reason and deletes the branch; cluster :paused wins; `seon.flow/start-graph!` resumes only under the intent. A table over the five intents. (hybrid-done-and-merge M12, reused P8) Close derivations in `next-actions`: `:cancel` (intent `:cancelled`: interrupt, await actual exit, then `close-tx` writes outcome `:closed` with end `:cancelled` and `:delete-branch`; no task for root, no end written by `seon.cluster.agent/follow!`), send-back, cap, stall, given-back and error closes with the end the branch holds at C; stop keeps the branch and the open assignment; pause is acknowledged by `clojure.core.async.flow/ping-proc`. Red proofs: `seon.cluster.db-flow-test/a-cancelled-assignment-closes-without-a-task-for-root` (`:closed`, end `:cancelled`, zero new root tasks) and the filed deftest tagged to this task (`each-intent-names-one-roster-state`). Cost: `close-tx` under 10 ms. After M5."
             :must-read #{seon.cluster.agent/start-flow! seon.cluster.agent/stop-flow! seon.flow/start-graph! seon.cluster.agent/plan seon.cluster.agent/apply-plan! seon.cluster.agent/db<->flow seon.turn/end-task-call seon.task/close! clojure.core.async.flow/ping-proc}
             :needs #{[:seon.task/sym seon.flow/an-ended-attempt-reaches-main-in-one-transaction-and-its-branch-is-deleted]}
             :evidence #{"docs/research/agent-platform/2026-10-12/hybrid-done-and-merge.md"}}
 #:seon.task{:name "disarm-splits-into-stop-and-cancel"
             :kind :function
             :intent "Card D3 (with R2). design-probes P3: `clojure.core.async.flow/stop` then `seon.flow/join-graph!` without `interrupt-turn!` exits after the current transform (Flow reads control with priority between transforms; a transform is never preempted), and `flow/pause` then `flow/ping-proc` under the same bound acknowledges the pass's end. So `seon.cluster.agent/stop-flow!` splits: a :stopped intent is `stop-graph!` minus the interrupt-turn! line, a :cancelled intent keeps it; the bound `:seon.config.agent/turn-completion-backstop-ms` covers a whole pass (its dial already adds the provider or evaluation limit); a pass past the bound gets today's typed join timeout with the entry kept. Proof: R2's table row for :stopped (seon.cluster.agent-test/each-intent-names-one-roster-state)."
             :must-read #{seon.cluster.agent/stop-flow! seon.flow/join-graph!}
             :evidence #{"docs/research/agent-platform/2026-10-11/runtime-follows-db.md" "docs/research/agent-platform/2026-10-11/design-probes.md"}}
 #:seon.task{:name "a-fresh-agents-environment-costs-one-fork"
             :kind :function
             :intent "Phase E3 of hybrid-immutable-env.md (H5 option B, decided). A fresh agent's environment is ONE `sci.core/fork` of the frozen program context (`seon.sci.eval/program-context`, phase E2) plus custody, print session, fresh kernel atoms and result objects: `seon.sci.eval/fork-for-agent` (value in, context out) replaces `seon.sci.eval/copy-base-ctx`, `seon.sci.eval/fork-cluster-ctx`, `seon.sci.eval/branch-ctx` and `seon.sci.eval/fork-for-turn`'s fresh path at `seon.cluster.agent/acquire-context!`, `seon.test`'s request world and `seon.cluster.boot`; the agent keeps the value it forked from; `:my.program/base-ctx` and `:seon.sci.eval/base-ctx` leave the handle. Lazy installs (`seon.sci.kernel/ensure-function!`) land in the agent's own fork; the proof records how many functions the loaded value leaves lazy, deciding whether the derivation installs them eagerly. Proof: seon.cluster.agent-test/a-fresh-agents-environment-costs-two-forks renamed a-fresh-agents-environment-costs-one-fork and tightened: `seon.sci.eval/inherit-var-bindings!` called once, `seon.sci.eval/acquire!` and `seon.sci.eval/fork-cluster-ctx` zero, no history reads for an agent that never ran, custody is the branch connection, environment leg under 1 ms and 1 MB beyond the lookup (14.5 ms and 9.1 MB with two forks, 25.6 ms and 16.6 MB with four). Files: src/seon/sci/eval.clj, src/seon/cluster/agent.clj (held by lane p4-branch-writes: a patch for root), src/seon/test.clj, src/seon/cluster/boot.clj, test/seon/cluster/agent_test.clj."
             :must-read #{seon.sci.eval/base-ctx seon.sci.eval/branch-ctx seon.sci.eval/fork-for-turn seon.sci.eval/fork-cluster-ctx seon.cluster.agent/acquire-context! seon.sci.kernel/ensure-function! seon.profile/measure}
             :evidence #{"docs/research/agent-platform/2026-10-12/hybrid-immutable-env.md" "docs/research/agent-platform/2026-10-11/hybrid-start-a-flow.md"}}
 #:seon.task{:name "a-received-message-is-written-on-the-recipients-branch-and-rendered-in-its-context"
             :kind :function
             :intent "Phase AW7 of hybrid-agent-world.md (Opus lane; beside AW4 and AW5). Messages are channels: the agent graph's mailbox proc (`seon.cluster.agent/mailbox-step`, today one payload-free wake in-port on a sliding-buffer 1) gains a second in-port :seon.agent/inbox, a bounded buffer carrying the message value (new dial :seon.config.agent/inbox-size, default 64, resources/seon/schemas/seon.config.agent.edn), registered in the registry beside the wake channel so `seon.cluster.agent/channels` answers it for `seon.cluster.message/send!`'s offer!. On an inbox message the :io transform writes the message on the agent's OWN connection with `seon.cluster.message/send-call` (identity :seon.message/id, so a repeat is a no-op) and then emits its :seon.agent/episode signal, so the next turn's `seon.context/current-state` renders the inbox from the agent's own branch as today. Nothing is written in transit, nothing relays through main, no other writer touches the branch; a crash between offer and write loses the message (channels are ephemeral), and the sender resends by the same id. Root's graph has the same mailbox, so messages to root are written on main by root's own proc. Proofs: this test (after one offer the recipient's branch holds the message by identity, main and the sender's branch do not, the episode fired once and current-state shows the message; offer -> write under 30 ms warm through seon.profile/measure); a-resent-message-with-the-same-id-writes-nothing (two offers, one datom set, two episodes); a-message-to-root-takes-the-same-path (root's mailbox writes it on main). Files: src/seon/cluster/agent.clj (mailbox-step, graph-definition, routing), resources/seon/schemas/seon.config.agent.edn, test/seon/cluster/agent_test.clj."
             :must-read #{seon.cluster.agent/mailbox-step seon.cluster.agent/graph-definition seon.cluster.agent/channels seon.cluster.message/send-call seon.context/current-state seon.profile/measure}
             :evidence #{"docs/research/agent-platform/2026-10-11/hybrid-agent-world.md"}}
 #:seon.task{:name "a-successor-starts-from-its-predecessors-program-context"
             :kind :function
             :intent "Phase E6 of hybrid-immutable-env.md (owner decision: a chain step forks its predecessor's end commit, reusing its SCI and db points; supersedes hybrid-agent-world.md's fork-from-main row and closes astra-overnight-review.md P1). `seon.cluster.agent/fork-attempt!` forks the predecessor's end commit persisted on the attempt (`:seon.task.attempt/fork-point`, H8), creates the successor's branch before `seon.task/delete-branch!` removes the predecessor's so Datahike retention owns the history, and the successor's program context is that commit's `seon.sci.eval/program-context` lookup: the predecessor's own value when its final recording changed no program row. The successor's private layer is fresh; a live value moves only by an explicit transfer. No missing-branch fallback to main and no row import. Proof: seon.cluster.agent-test/a-successor-starts-from-its-predecessors-program-context: the predecessor changes a function and ends, main advances differently, the successor executes the predecessor's definition and reads its end facts, none of the predecessor's private Vars is present, `seon.sci.eval/derive-base-ctx` and `seon.sci.eval/interpret-overlay!` are called zero times, environment leg under 2 ms through `seon.profile/measure`; repeated after emptying the kind on a disposable store. After H7, H8, phase E5 and lane p4-branch-writes' release. Files: src/seon/cluster/agent.clj, src/seon/task.clj, test/seon/cluster/agent_test.clj."
             :must-read #{seon.cluster.agent/fork-attempt! seon.cluster.agent/start-flow! seon.task/delete-branch! seon.sci.eval/base-ctx seon.cluster.registry/fork! seon.profile/measure}
             :evidence #{"docs/research/agent-platform/2026-10-12/hybrid-immutable-env.md" "docs/research/agent-platform/2026-10-11/hybrid-agent-world.md" "docs/research/agent-platform/2026-10-12/astra-overnight-review.md"}}
 #:seon.task{:name "a-successor-whose-proof-its-predecessor-wrote-starts"
  :kind :function
  :intent "Phase P5 of hybrid-step-semantics.md (F3). A chain step whose proof its predecessor wrote cannot start: `seon.cluster.agent/plan-facts` judges a ready leaf valid by `seon.task/task-edges` on main, but the predecessor's proof lives on its attempt branch, so the leaf is :invalid, `seon.cluster.agent/plan` writes the problem task seon.task/invalid-task-<handle> and never attempts it. Observed on default 2319152dc (chain experiment 1): trailing-prose-implement became ready when Design+Prove ended :done and was refused for want of a proof that its fork point held. Correct: validity reads `seon.task/obligations` on the value the leaf will fork from (the done needs' end commits in `:seon.flow.plan/basis` opened as a database, else main), cached by commit id, the same value its first pass proves on. An :implement step needs its own proofs at that value; a :prove step needs its own proofs or a successor declaring it in :seon.task/needs (the product is what it will write, not an admission input); `seon.cluster.agent/write-problem!`'s hardcoded 'names no proof' intent becomes the failed check as data. Proof: seon.cluster.db-flow-test/a-successor-whose-proof-its-predecessor-wrote-starts (chain-experiment-1-proofs.patch). Files: src/seon/cluster/agent.clj, test/seon/cluster/db_flow_test.clj."
  :must-read #{seon.cluster.agent/plan-facts seon.cluster.agent/plan seon.task/obligations datahike.api/commit-as-db seon.cluster.agent/write-problem!}
  :evidence #{"docs/research/agent-platform/2026-10-12/step-p5-p6-landing.md" "docs/research/agent-platform/2026-10-12/hybrid-step-semantics.md" "docs/research/agent-platform/2026-10-12/chain-experiment-1.md"}}
 #:seon.task{:name "a-capped-attempt-has-no-relaunch"
             :kind :function
             :intent "(hybrid-done-and-merge M12, reused P9) A closed assignment of a terminal class (`:capped`, `:stalled`, `:given-back`, `:error`) is relaunched only by root's fact: new `my.root/relaunch!` writes `:seon.task.attempt/relaunch` (its reason, optional `:seon.task.attempt/relaunch-fork` naming a commit) and `seon.task/ready` reads 'an ended assignment of a terminal class with no relaunch' as not ready, so assignment n+1 forks main or the named commit; no end is rewritten and nothing retries by itself. Red proof: `seon.cluster.db-flow-test/a-capped-attempt-relaunches-only-by-roots-fact` (capped gives a problem and no assignment 2; relaunch gives assignment 2; a relaunch naming a commit forks that commit). Files: `src/seon/cluster/agent.clj`, `src/my/root.clj`, `src/seon/task.clj` (the ready rule). After M5."
             :must-read #{seon.task/ready seon.task/ready-rules seon.cluster.agent/plan seon.turn/end-task-call seon.task/create! my.root/merge!}
             :needs #{[:seon.task/sym seon.cluster.agent/each-intent-names-one-roster-state]}
             :evidence #{"docs/research/agent-platform/2026-10-12/chain-experiment-2.md" "docs/research/agent-platform/2026-10-12/hybrid-done-and-merge.md"}}
 #:seon.task{:name "the-real-chain-runs-with-no-nudges"
             :kind :function
             :intent "(hybrid-done-and-merge M-final) After M1-M18 are merged and default restarts on that HEAD, the chain test `seon.cluster.db-flow-test/a-green-first-step-ends-itself-and-its-end-starts-the-next-from-its-commit` is extended to Design+Prove (an expected red product) then Implement then auto-merge, with no hand end edits, no copying of proofs to main and no successor launched by hand (injected provider replies); then chain experiment 1's subject (`seon.sci.forgive/segments`, trailing prose) reruns paid with the same instructions: about 5 + 4 calls, at most one send-back, merged into main with zero nudges. Files: `test/seon/cluster/db_flow_test.clj`; the run's note under `docs/research/agent-platform/`. After M18 (`seon.vocabulary/the-merge-path-names-no-retired-identifier`)."
